Hi! Got a response:

After a thorough analysis, our primary concern remains the exposure of Qminder to liability for data privacy violations in a way which is not aligned to our liability limitation principles. While we understand your practical concerns, this limitation – i.e. amount of Service Fees paid for 12 months – has been carefully considered in the context of the overall agreement set-up. Further, we consider this limitation to be industry standard, i.e. reasonable and fair towards both parties. Raising such a liability standard cap, as well as making it “per-claim”, would trigger a number of fundamental considerations from our side, as the impact on the risk distribution as compared to the standard set-up would be material.

{quote}First and foremost, having such modified liability limitation clause would have operational business impact to us. Additionally, we are keen to avoid setting a precedent (even if confidential) that would deviate from our approach to offer comparable terms to all of our customers. Overall, the proposed “per-claim” cap poses significant risks under our risk management framework and risks our business sustainability, making it unacceptable in the proposed form.{quote}

{quote}{quote}

{quote}We further emphasise that, taking into account the nature of personal data processed, we believe it is disproportionate to justify Qminder being exposed to such high liability. We are primarily involved in processing data subjects’ names, contact details, other data related to waitlists and such appointments (Article 2 of the Schedule 1). Thus, this personal data processed for providing the services to Bolt is, in our view, not considered to be high risk or sensitive (nor considered as special categories of personal data). Therefore, we are positive that our standard general liability cap is reasonable and balanced.{quote}

{quote} {quote}

{quote}Additionally, Qminder provides a configurable [*data retention functionality*|https://help.qminder.com/configure-data-retention-settings] that allows for automatic removal of personal data after a predefined time period, further reducing potential data exposure and associated risk.{quote}

{quote} {quote}

{quote}We also refer to clause 21.2, which excludes any liability limitation in case of, among others, “personal injury caused by Qminder’s negligence [...]”. To the best of our understanding, “personal injury” would also include cases where there is significant harm regarding, e.g., a person's reputation (leading to emotional distress). Meaning that in such a case, Qminder would be in any case subject to unlimited liability.{quote}

{quote} {quote}

{quote}We look forward to your considerations regarding the above. In any case, we are open to further discussions to resolve this matter in finding a common ground which addresses the concerns of both parties.{quote}

As I’ve sent the legal basis in Spanish by mistake. Here is the version in English: 

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
Article 46bis of the Code of Criminal Procedure stipulates that cooperation is mandatory for any operator of an electronic communications network and for any person who, within Belgian territory and in any way, makes available or offers a service consisting of transmitting signals via electronic communications networks, or which consists of allowing users to obtain, receive or distribute information via an electronic communications network. This also includes the provider of an electronic communication service. Any person who, by virtue of their position, has knowledge of the measure or cooperates in it, shall be bound to secrecy. Any violation of secrecy shall be punished in accordance with Article 458 of the Penal Code. Any person who refuses to communicate the data or does not communicate it in real time or, where appropriate, at the time specified in the summons, shall be punished by a fine of between twenty-six euros and ten thousand euros.
