*“Norway*
_Report from our correspondent Fred Kenneth Degerstrøm, Freelance Tax Adviser, Norway_
*Norway to Introduce New Reporting Obligations for Digital Platforms*
The parliament has adopted the government’s proposed amendments to the Tax Administration Act. The changes would implement the OECD’s Digital Platform Information (DPI) standard into Norwegian law and introduce new reporting obligations for digital platforms that facilitate the rental of real estate and transport equipment, and the sale of services.The key elements of the proposed amendments are the following:
–    expanded reporting obligations: digital platforms will have to report information on both Norwegian and foreign taxpayers who use the platform to rent out real estate or vehicles, or to sell services;
–    international exchange of information: information about foreign taxpayers will be shared with their home countries, while Norway will receive information about Norwegian users of foreign platforms;
–    exemptions: the sale of goods will be excluded from the new rules but may be considered in the future;
–    entry into force: the rules will take effect on 1 January 2026, with the first reporting due in 2027; and
